---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import { MotionLoopReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-loop.tsx';
import { MotionLoopAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-loop.ts';
import MotionLoopSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-loop.svelte';
import reactSource from '@/examples/react/motion-loop.tsx?raw';
import angularSource from '@/examples/angular/motion-loop.ts?raw';
import svelteSource from '@/examples/svelte/motion-loop.svelte?raw';
import { buttonClass, demoClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage title="Motion: reveal, scrub and loop" description="View motions that play when an element scrolls into view, motions linked to the scroll position, and loops that can be paused.">
  <h1>Reveal, scrub and loop</h1>
  <p>Three verbs follow time or the scroll position rather than a presence. A <code>reveal</code> plays when its element scrolls into view, a <code>scrub</code> links its keyframes to the scroll, and a <code>loop</code> plays forever. The verb sets the trigger, so each one is written one way only.</p>

  <h2>Reveal</h2>
  <p>A reveal waits in its first frame while its element is out of view, and plays when the element scrolls into view. An element counts as in view as soon as any part of it is in the top 90 % of the viewport. An element that is in view when it is bound is left in its natural state. The preset <code>rise</code> fades in while rising 24 px:</p>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="rise">…</div>

import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

defineMotion('rise-again', {
  verb: 'reveal',
  keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, y: 24 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0 }],
  stagger: 'normal',
  repeat: 'always',
});`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>stagger</code> (<code>tight</code>, <code>normal</code> or <code>loose</code>) and <code>origin</code> work as for lists: elements that enter the view together start one step apart, in visual order.</li>
    <li><code>repeat</code>: <code>once</code> (the default) plays the reveal the first time the element enters the view; <code>always</code> puts the element back in its first frame when it leaves the view, and plays it again on each entry.</li>
    <li><code>axis</code>: <code>block</code> (the default) watches the vertical view, <code>inline</code> the horizontal one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><code>play()</code> on the binding replays a reveal from its first frame. The reveal is live on the <a href="/motion/demo">demo</a>.</p>

  <h2>Scrub</h2>
  <p>A scrub links its keyframes to the scroll position: as the element crosses the viewport its keyframes play forward, and scrolling back plays them backward. The preset <code>parallax</code> moves its element from 48 px below its place to 48 px above it.</p>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="parallax">…</div>

import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

defineMotion('drift-in', {
  verb: 'scrub',
  keyframes: [{ opacity: 0, y: 48 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0 }],
  range: 'enter',
});`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>range</code> chooses the part of the scroll the keyframes span: <code>enter</code> (the element entering the viewport), <code>cross</code> (the default: its crossing of the viewport), <code>exit</code> (leaving it), or <code>contain</code> (its containment in the viewport).</li>
    <li><code>axis</code>: <code>block</code> (the default) follows the vertical scroll, <code>inline</code> the horizontal one.</li>
    <li>The keyframes are interpolated linearly across the range. Before the range the first keyframe holds, and after it the last one. A scrub has no timing and no presence.</li>
    <li><code>--udx-motion-scale</code> scales the distances of the keyframes (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>rotate</code>, and a scale's distance from 1). It is read when the scrub starts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The scroll link is made by the browser, on a view timeline. <code>supportsViewTimeline()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion/continuous</code>, tells whether the browser has one. Where it has none, the element keeps its stylesheet state and nothing is written inline.</p>

  <h2>Loop</h2>
  <p>A loop plays its keyframes forever, from the moment it is bound. It pauses by itself while its element is out of view, and plays again when the element comes back. The presets <code>rotate</code> (a full turn each second), <code>breathe</code> (swells to 104 % and back) and <code>blink</code> (one dim flash each second) are loops.</p>
  <pre><code>{`<span data-motion="rotate" class="spinner"></span>

import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

defineMotion('spin', {
  verb: 'loop',
  keyframes: [{ rotate: 0 }, { rotate: 360 }],
  pause: 'control',
});`}</code></pre>
  <p>WCAG 2.2.2 asks that moving, blinking or scrolling content which starts by itself, lasts more than five seconds and runs beside other content can be paused, stopped or hidden, unless it is essential. A loop therefore states in its definition how it is paused:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>pause: 'control'</code>: the page offers a visible control that pauses the loop. The presets use it.</li>
    <li><code>pause: 'essential'</code> with a <code>reason</code>: the loop is the content itself, such as an indeterminate progress indicator. The validator requires the reason, and refuses it on a <code>control</code> loop.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it</h2>
  <p>The spinner is a loop with a control. The button pauses it, by the attribute <code>data-motion-paused</code>, and plays it again.</p>
  <div class={demoClass}>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
      <span data-motion="rotate" data-loop-demo class="block size-8 rounded-full border-4 border-primary border-t-transparent"></span>
      <button type="button" class={buttonClass} data-loop-toggle>Pause spinner</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{`// The page pauses the loop, from the attribute or from script
spinner.setAttribute('data-motion-paused', '');
spinner.removeAttribute('data-motion-paused');

getMotion(spinner, 'loop')?.pause();
getMotion(spinner, 'loop')?.play(); // clears the pause`}</code></pre>

  <h2>In the frameworks</h2>
  <p>The loop takes a <code>paused</code> option: React <code>useMotion('rotate', &#123; paused &#125;)</code>, Angular <code>[udxMotionPaused]</code>, Svelte <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('rotate', () =&gt; (&#123; paused &#125;))&#125;</code>.</p>
  <Code codes={{ react: reactSource, angular: angularSource, svelte: svelteSource }}>
    <MotionLoopReact slot="react" client:only="react" />
    <MotionLoopAngular slot="angular" client:load />
    <MotionLoopSvelte slot="svelte" client:only="svelte" />
  </Code>

  <h2>Timing of a loop</h2>
  <p>A loop has no spring: its <code>timing</code> is a curve only, <code>&#123; duration, easing &#125;</code>. Without <code>timing</code> it runs for one second, linearly. <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> scales its duration and the distances of its keyframes, and 0 stops it.</p>

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code>, a loop does not play: the element keeps its stylesheet state. A reveal shows its element at once, and a scrub stays still. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Accessibility</a>.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A scrub is driven by the browser's view timeline. Where the browser has none, the scroll link is not made (see above).</li>
    <li>Under reduced motion a scrub is still, and turning reduced motion on stops the scroll link.</li>
    <li>A loop with <code>pause: 'control'</code> needs a visible control on the page to be pausable.</li>
  </ul>

  <script>
    document.addEventListener('click', event => {
      if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;
      const toggle = event.target.closest<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-loop-toggle]');
      if (!toggle) return;
      const spinner = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-loop-demo]');
      if (!spinner) return;
      const paused = !spinner.hasAttribute('data-motion-paused');
      spinner.toggleAttribute('data-motion-paused', paused);
      toggle.textContent = paused ? 'Play spinner' : 'Pause spinner';
    });
  </script>
</MotionPage>
